Pyro patch 0032: a medium or short date and a time are joined as ICU 77 joins them.

ICU 78 (CLDR 48, CLDR-18370) joins a date and a time with the "atTime" pattern of the date's
length by default. For a medium or short date that pattern now carries a word in some locales:
English `{1} 'at' {0}` ("Oct 1 at 13:05", "Oct 1, 2026 at 1:05 PM"), French `à`, Polish `o`.
ICU 77, which Chromium ships, joined them with the standard pattern: "Oct 1, 13:05",
"Oct 1, 2026, 1:05 PM". macOS 27's ICU is 78.1, which JavaScriptCore links there.

Where the atTime pattern of a medium or short Gregorian date quotes a literal and the standard one
is not a bare `{1} {0}`, the standard one joins them (`dateTimeJoiningPatternBeforeCLDR48`,
IntlCache.cpp). Where CLDR 48 moved a word out of the standard pattern into the atTime one, as
Finnish "klo", it left the standard one bare, and ICU 77 joined with the word: that stays. The
replacement happens in the shared date-time pattern
generator (`udatpg_setDateTimeFormatForStyle`, for skeletons), and for `dateStyle` with `timeStyle`
(the date and time patterns joined again in `initializeDateTimeFormat`). Long and full dates keep
ICU's pattern ("October 1, 2026 at 1:05 PM", as in Chromium), and so do the locales whose atTime
pattern has no word (Portuguese `{1}, {0}`, whose standard one became `{1} {0}`). Against Chromium
147 (ICU 77): of 630 strings (45 locales, the date and time styles and 6 skeletons), 22 now equal
Chromium's (English, French, Polish) and none differs that did not; 120 locales in 4 medium and
short formats show the same. What still differs is CLDR 47 against 48 data (month and day period
names, short years, Chromium's trimmed locales), which no joining pattern fixes.

Patterns are read from the locale's `calendar/gregorian/DateTimePatterns` and
`DateTimePatterns%atTime` (or its nearest parent's). An ICU before 78 (Linux's bundled one) and
a non-Gregorian calendar keep ICU's pattern. Not Darwin-only.

